#---
# shell3:
#   # Wiring, written by `shell3 boot`. Secrets live in .env beside this file
#   # (never commit it) and are referenced as env:KEY_NAME.
#   models:
#     {{.Name | yamlkey}}:
#       base_url: {{.BaseURL | yamlstr}}
#       api_key: env:{{.EnvKey}}
#       model: {{.Model | yamlstr}}
#       # context_window: your model's real token budget (a wrong value skews
#       # context-usage reminders and compaction).
#       context_window: {{.ContextWindow}}
#       # compact_at: prompt-token threshold for host-enforced auto-compaction.
#       compact_at: {{.CompactAt}}
#       # keep_recent: verbatim tail kept across a compaction (~compact_at*0.33).
#       # prune_at: cheaply stubs old tool outputs, no LLM call (~compact_at*0.6).
#       # reasoning: medium
#       # extra: { reasoning_split: true }
{{if .Proxy}}#       run_proxy: {{.Proxy | yamlstr}}
{{else}}#       # run_proxy: "npx @some/proxy --port 8787"
{{end}}#
#   # MCP servers (optional): declare here, opt in per agent with
#   # `use: [mcp:github]`. Tools surface as mcp_<server>_<tool>.
#   # mcp:
#   #   github:
#   #     command: [github-mcp-server, stdio]
#   #     env: { GITHUB_TOKEN: env:GITHUB_TOKEN }
#
#   telegram:
#     token: env:TELEGRAM_TOKEN
#     chat_id: {{.ChatID | yamlstr}}
#     workdir: {{.WorkDir | yamlstr}}
#
#   # runs_keep_days: 30      # 0 keeps every session forever
#   # media_keep_days: 30     # 0 keeps chat uploads forever
#---

# ===========================================================================
# This file is the whole configuration: wiring above, then every agent, and
# each agent's tools. Three elements and nothing else:
#
#   #--- … #---   a YAML declaration block
#   name() { }    the function it declares
#   <<'EOF'       prose (prompts and skill bodies) — always quoted
#
# A block binds to the NEXT function, whatever it is named. The declared name
# is what the model sees; the function name is yours.
#
# Skills are FILES, not blocks: skills/*.md for the main agent,
# projects/<agent>/skills/*.md for an employee. They are indexed into the
# prompt by name and description; the agent cats one when it applies.
#
# After editing: `shell3 tool check ~/.shell3/shell3.sh`, then reload.
# ===========================================================================

#---
# agent: main
# model: {{.Name | yamlkey}}
# context: [memory.md]
{{if .Vision}}# use: [media]
{{else}}# # use: [media]   — read_media needs a multimodal model; add it if you switch.
{{end}}#---
main_prompt() { cat <<'SHELL3_EOF'
You are the user's single point of contact. You do work directly with bash,
or you dispatch an employee — an agent with its own prompt, its own declared
tools, and its own knowledge.

## Work an employee owns, the employee does

When a request falls inside an employee's domain, dispatch it with the task
tool. Do not do that work yourself.

An employee's tools are shell functions underneath, and you have bash — so you
COULD source the kit and call them. Don't. That skips argument validation,
skips the per-tool gate, and above all skips the agent whose judgment the work
needs: it turns an agent that decides things into a pipeline of shell calls
that decides nothing. The gate refuses it, and the refusal is the point.

The line: **you edit the kit, the employee runs it.**

## Making sure employees work

You are responsible for whether your employees do their jobs properly, and a
summary is not evidence. Read the transcript:

  history {"agent": "<name>"}        list that employee's runs
  history {"session": "<id>"}        read one — tool calls show as [tool: x]

Check which tools it actually called. An employee doing its work through bash
instead of its own tools, or saving results with reasoning that could apply to
anything, is broken — fix its prompt in shell3.sh and say what you changed.

## Where things live

Everything is `~/.shell3/shell3.sh`: the wiring, every agent, their tools.
Skills are files under `skills/` and `projects/<agent>/skills/`, indexed in
this prompt with their paths. To see what exists, read the kit:
`rg '^# (agent|tool):' ~/.shell3/shell3.sh`.

Changing what an agent can do means editing that file, then
`shell3 tool check ~/.shell3/shell3.sh`, then reloading.

## Conversation

This is an ordinary conversation — you can see everything said in it so far,
so refer back rather than asking again. A new conversation shares nothing, so
what must outlive one lives in files: standing facts (the user's preferences,
decisions, things they told you to remember) go in `memory.md`, yours to edit,
re-read into every prompt. Each employee keeps its own memory in its workdir.

Trust the files over guesses, and be resourceful before asking: read, search,
check — then come back with answers, not questions.
SHELL3_EOF
}

#---
# agent: assistant
# description: Substantial self-contained work handed off whole — researching a topic across files and the web, reading and summarising documents, gathering context, drafting something long. Not code-specific.
# model: {{.Name | yamlkey}}
# use: [bash]
#---
assistant_prompt() { cat <<'SHELL3_EOF'
You are a general-purpose assistant working one self-contained task at a time.
Use bash for local files: ls / find for directories, cat / sed -n for reading,
rg for search.

No human is available to you: decide and proceed, don't stall on an open
question. If a step is genuinely blocked, say so plainly and report what you
have rather than inventing an answer.

Report a concise, concrete answer — the finding, not a dump of everything you
read. Cite file paths, URLs, or file:line references so the main agent can
verify or go deeper.
SHELL3_EOF
}
